        "title": "1.3 Integers",
        "theory": "1.3 Integers\n\nAdil is a shopkeeper. He sells honey. A bottle costs him Rs 500. He sells it for Rs 501, 502, or 510, etc. Thus, he gets some additional money per bottle. The additional money is his profit. Now, what will be his profit if he sells a bottle for Rs 500? In this case, he will get no\n\nadditional money and thus no profit. We may say that his profit, in this case, will be \"0\"\n\nrupees.\n\nNow suppose that Adil sells a honey bottle for Rs 499, Rs 498, or Rs 490, etc. In this case, he will get no profit. In fact, he will be at a loss of 1, 2, or 10 rupees, etc. Can you tell his loss if he sells a bottle for Rs 500?\n\nIf the sale price is the same as the cost price, he Note it down\n\nwill make no profit as well as no loss. This situation can be taken as the starting point \"0\". So, we conclude that;\n\ni. When sale price = cost price, this indicates the starting point '0'.\n\nii. When sale price > cost price, this indicates profit. The amount of profit is expressed with\n\nthe positive numbers +1, +2, or +10, etc.\n\n1. When we fix a point as the starting point or base, the number above the point is expressed with the + sign and the number below the starting point is expressed with the - sign.\n\nii. The base or starting point is indicated by 10(+1) is 1 above the base, and (-1) is 1 below the base.\n\nii 'O' is neither positive nor negative.\n\niii. When sale price < cost price, this indicates loss. The amount of loss is expressed with the negative signs-1,-2, or-10, etc.\n\nAll natural numbers are positive numbers. How can we express the negative numbers on the number line?\n\nWhole numbers are usually expressed at the right of '0' on the number line and the negative numbers are expressed at the left side of 0 on the number line as;\n\n-3\n\n-2 -1\n\nNote it down\n\nCarefully observe the number line, we can find that every positive number has the\n\ncorresponding negative number.\n\nIn the absence of negative numbers, we do not necessarily write + sign with positive numbers but in the presence of negative numbers, we need to write the + or signs with the numbers to distinguish them.\n\nThe number 0, positive natural numbers and negative of natural numbers are known as integers.\n\nIntegers are categorized into three major types: ii. Negative integers\n\ni. Positive integers\n\niii. O (neutral integer)",
