        "theory": "4.1 Rate and Average Rate\n\nA rate is a ratio that compares two quantities with different units of measure.\n\nRates are used almost every day. For example, unit price of items, speed, service charges etc. are often given as rates The unit of distance is metre and unit of time is hour.\n\nNate it down\n\nWe usually use the word \"per\" to mention rate. The symbol for rate is \"/\" e.g. \"Rs 250 per kg\" is same as \"Rs 250/Kg\"\n\nWe compare the weight/mass of objects with money etc. A rate is a comparison of quantities of different kinds with different units, so it is important to mention the units used in both quantities. Different quantities can be used together to form a rate. For example, 89 km/h, 50 jumps/min, Rs 250/100 g. Rs 965/kg. Rs 280/litre).\n\nObserve these examples:",
