        "title": "5.1 Profit, Loss and Discount",
        "theory": "5.1 Profit, Loss and Discount\n\nWhen we buy something from the market, we pay an amount for the item(s) we buy. This amount is the cost price of the item(s).\n\nCost Price:\n\nThe original price (purchasing price) of an item is called cost price. In short, we write it as C.P. Selling Price:\n\nThe price at which an item is sold is its selling price. In short, we write it as S.P.\n\nProfit:\n\nThe difference between the selling price (S.P) and the cost price (C.P), when the selling price.\n\nis greater than the cost price, is called profit.\n\nLoss:\n\nThe difference between the cost price (CP) and the selling price (S.P), when the cost price is greater than the selling price, is called loss.\n\nPercentage Profit or Loss:\n\nPrevious Knowledge check\n\nExpress 48 kg out of 300 kg as percentage.\n\nFind 17% of 234.\n\nCompare 45 out of 120 and 65 out of 135 as percentage.\n\nProfit = selling price - cost price\n\nProfit = S.P-C.P\n\nLoss = cost price - selling price\n\nLoss = C.P-S.P\n\nIf we want to find the percentage of a profit or loss, we use the following formulas.\n\nProfit\n\nProfit % =\n\n* 100%\n\ncost price",
