        "title": "6.1.1 Representation of a set",
        "theory": "6.1.1 Representation of a set\n\nQuick Check\n\nWrite 2 sets of your own choice and list their elements.\n\nWe can represent a set in more than one form. Here we will discuss the following forms: 1. Descriptive Form\n\n2. Tabular Form\n\nWe already know about descriptive and tabular forms of representing a set. Let's recall it and then learn about another form of representing a set which is known as set builder form. 1. Descriptive Form\n\nNote it down\n\nElements of a set may be finite or infinite.\n\nIn descriptive form a set is represented in the form of statements using well-defined words. Examples:\n\na) A = Set of composite numbers between 10 and 30.\n\nb) F = Set of even numbers less than or equal to 50.\n\nc) T = Set of the days of a week having 6 letters.\n\nIn the above examples, we can see that all sets are written in the form of well-defined statements. These sets are in descriptive form.\n\n2. Tabular Form\n\nIn tabular form, elements of the set are placed within the curly\n\nbrackets {} separated by commas.\n\nNote it down\n\nTabular form is also called Roster form.",
