        "theory": "7.3.2 Types of Polynomials\n\nThere are different types of polynomials which can be categorized with respect to variables, or terms. 1. With Respect to Number of Variables\n\na) Polynomial in one Variable\n\nA polynomial that consists of one variable is known as\n\na polynomial in one variable.\n\nQuick Check\n\nWhat is the degree of these polynomials?\n\na) x2+3x-28\n\nb) x + 12x2 + 35\n\nc)-56\n\nFor example: 2x+3x+1, 5x+x+1, 2x+1 are the polynomials in one variable.\n\nb) Polynomial in two Variables\n\nA polynomial that consists of two variables is known as a polynomial in two variables. For example: 5x+3y+1, 7a+b+1, Bu+v are the polynomials in two variables.\n\nc) Polynomial in more than two Variables\n\nA polynomial that consists of more than two variables is known as a polynomial in more than two variables.\n\nFor example:\n\na) 5x+7y+z is the polynomial in three variables.\n\nb) 6a2+3b+8c+d is the polynomial in four variables\n\n2. With Respect to Number of Terms\n\na) Monomials\n\nPolynomials which contain only one term are known as monomials. For example, 3x, 4y, and 6a2 are all examples of monomials.\n\nb) Binomials\n\nPolynomials which contain only two terms are known as binomials.\n\nFor example: 4x+3 and a'-b are examples of binomials.\n\nc) Trinomials\n\nPolynomials which contain three terms are known as trinomials. For example: 3u+5v+1 and x2-7y+1 are examples of trinomials.",
        "heading": "7.3.2 Types of Polynomials",
