        "title": "7.6 Simplification of Algebraic Expressions",
        "theory": "7.6 Simplification of Algebraic Expressions\n\nTo simplify algebraic expressions involving brackets, we follow the same order of preference as we do in arithmetic by following the BODMAS rule in order of operations so that no miscalculations are made.\n\nSimilarly in algebraic expressions brackets are used to simplify them. Let's consider the blowing examples.\n\nGample 1:\n\nSimplify; a'{(a —b) − ( a + b)}\n\nQuick Check\n\nSimplify the following.\n\na) [3d (7a-b)+ ((a - b) + (a-3b)}]\n\n-\n\nb) [6x(x2 + xy +2) — [(4-2) (5x-y)}]\n\nSolution:\n\nla-b)-(a+b))\n\nd\\a-b-a-b}\n\nd-26)\n\n-2b\n\nFirst solve the operation within brackets\n\nNow add and subtract the like terms\n\nNext multiply the terms\n\nWrite different questions related to BODMAS on the board and ask the students to simplify algebraic expressions according to the rule.",
