        "id": "7.7",
        "title": "7.7 Algebraic Identities",
        "theory": "7.7 Algebraic Identities\n\nAn equation is true conditionally, whereas the identity is always true. We know that an equation is true for specific values of the variable(s) involved. On the other hand, an identity is true for all values of the variable(s) involved. For example, 2x+5=9 is an equation. It is true only if x = 2. It cannot be true for values of x other than 1.\n\nAlgebraic Proof:\n\nIdentity I: (a + b)2 = a2 + 2ab + b2\n\n(a + b)2 = a2 + 2ab + b2\n\nProof:\n\nLHS. = (a + b)\n\n=(a+b) (a + b)\n\n= a + ab + ba + b2\n\n= d2 + ab + ab + b2\n\n= d2+2ab+b2\n\nHence proved (a + b)2 = a2 + 2ab + b2\n\nThus LHS. = RHS.",
