        "title": "Example 1",
        "problem": "Arabic world \"al-jabr\" which means\nthe reunion of broken parts. This word appeared in the book, \"Hisab al-Jabr wal-Muqabala\" written around the year 825 AD by the Muslim mathematician Muhammad\nibn Musa al-Khwarizmi (780-850\nAD). He is known as the \"Father of Algebra\".\nIf the length of one side of a square is given by y, the perimeter of the square will be given by 4xy. Here, the variable y (for the length of one side of a square) is used to derive the general rule or formula for the perimeter of a square. For any value of y, we can find the perimeter of the square using the general formula.\nSentences and Statements\nIn our daily conversation, we use different sets of words to communicate with each other. For example:\na) My name is Saad. b) We go to school at 8 o'clock.\nc) What are you doing? d) Please do your work.\nIn each of the above examples, all the words altogether are conveying full meaning leaving no confusion or ambiguity. Such sets of words are known as sentences. A sentence which is true according to the given condition is called a true sentence and a sentence which is false according to the given condition is called a false sentence. Every sentence which is either true or false is known as a statement. A statement cannot be both true and false at\nthe same time.\nGive an introduction of Muhammad Ibn Musa Al Khwarizmi as he was the first person to treat Algebra as an independent discipline and introduced the methods of reduction and balancing the equation. The term algebra itself comes from the title of his book, he has been described as the \"father of algebra\".\nConsider the following sentences and read them carefully.\na) 9 is less than 6.\nb) 4 plus 9 is equal to 13.\nc) Keep it up.\nd) 3 times 3 is 9.\ne) Lahore is the capital of Pakistan.\nIn these sentences it is clear that sentences (b) and (d) are true whereas (a) and\n(e) are false but (c) is neither true nor false. All of these are statements except (c) Open and Closed Sentences\nA mathematical sentence is called a closed sentence if that is known to be either true or false while an open sentence has variables whose values are not known.\nLook at the sentences below:\na) 5+= 12 b) 6-0 2c) 0+4 = 24 ·\nWhere D is an unknown value.\nHere we cannot say that the statements a, b and c\nQuick Check\nFind out whether the following are true or false:\na) 4 is an odd number.\nare true or false, because we do not know the value of D. by Difference of 2 and 5 is 7.\nA statement (or sentence) that does not include enough information required to decide whether it is true or false is known as an open statement.\nc) 14 is the multiple of both 7 and 2.\nIn the first example if we replace with the number '7', it becomes true.\nSimilarly, we can find the values of will be called a closed sentence.\nthat make (b) and (c) true. In such cases, the sentence\nExpressions, Equations and Inequalities\nAn algebraic expression is a combination of variables, constants, coefficients, exponents and symbols of operations i.e. + and\nFor example:\na) 3x\nb) 9 c) 5x+4y d) 2a-3b+ 4, etc.\nAn equation is a mathematical statement that shows the equality of two expressions while an inequality shows that an expression is less than or more than the other. An equation shows the equality of two variables while an inequality shows the inequality of two variables\nEACHING\n?\nExplain the concept of algebraic expressions to the students by writing a few expressions on the board and asking them to label its parts. For further practice ask them to visit https://www.khanacademy.org/math/algebra-basics/alg-basics-algebraic-expressions?t=practice\nConstant\nA constant is a number without a variable.\nIn 5+x=7, the letter x is the variable as it represents an unknown value. But, 5 and 7 are representing their fixed value. So, 5 and 7 are constants.\nCoefficient\nA number which is multiplied by a variable is called a coefficient. For example: 4x means 4 times x. Here, x is a variable and 4 is the\ncoefficient.\nExponent (power/index)\nExponent\n5x-6\nCoefficient\nConstant\nOperator\nVariable\nExponent is the number which represents repeated multiplication of the same variable. For example: In x, 2 is an exponent or power of variable x.\nTerms of an expression\nIn an algebraic expression, the variables or numbers which are separated by operators (+ or -) are called the terms of the expression.\nIn the expression 4a + 9b-c+ 2:4a, 9b, -c and 2 are the four terms of the expression.\nLike or Unlike Terms of an Algebraic Expression\nIn an algebraic expression, the terms whose variables as well as the exponents of those variables are the same are called like terms. The\ncoefficient of the like terms may or may not be the Note it down)\nsame. Like terms can be reduced to a single term through addition and subtraction. On the other hand, unlike terms are those terms whose variables are different or they have the same variables but with different exponents. Unlike terms cannot be added and subtracted together. Examples:\na) 3x-4y+5x\nIn an algebraic expression, the terms whose\nvariables as well as the exponents of those\nvariables are the same are called like terms.\nThe coefficients of the like terms may or may\nnot be the same.\nHere 3x and 5x are like terms as they have the same variable.\nb) 5a2 - 2a+3a2\nHere 502 and 302 are like terms. In the term 2a, the variable is the same but its exponent is 1 which is different from the exponents of the other terms. So, 2a and 5a2 as well as 2a and\n3a2 are unlike terms.",
