        "id": "8.1.2",
        "title": "8.1.2 Properties of Equality",
        "theory": "8.1.2 Properties of Equality\n\nAddition Property of Equality.\n\nIf the same number is added to both sides of an\n\nequation, the equation still remains equal. In general, for\n\nall numbers x, y, and z: If x=y, then x + z = y + z\n\nSubtraction Property of Equality.\n\nPrevious Knowledge Check\n\nIdentify and seperate out equation and expression.\n\na) y-2=5\n\nb) 2L + B\n\nc) a+b+c\n\nd) 1+9y=x\n\nIf the same number is subtracted from both sides of an equation, the equation still remains equal. In general, for all numbers x, y, and z. If x=y, then x-z = y-z.\n\nRecall a few basic concepts about linear equations which they have leant in previous grade. Then ask some concept related question or take a short quiz about the concepts. This will help the student recall their prior learning.\n\nMultiplication Property of Equality:\n\nIf both sides of an equation are multiplied by the same number, the equation still remains equal. In general, for all numbers x, y, and z: If x = y, then xxz = yxz.\n\nDivision Property of Equality:\n\nIf both sides of an equation are divided by the same non-zero number, the equation still remains equal. In general, for all numbers x, y, and z where z 0: If x = y, then\n\nI+Z=y+Z\n\nA linear equation may be in different forms. We will discuss the following forms here.\n\nii) x + b = c\n\ni) ax = b\n\niv) ax + b = ∞x + d\n\n(ax + b) m\n\n(ax + b) n\n\niii) ax + b = c\n\nWhere x is the variable and a, b, c and d are numbers. Here (i) and (ii) can be solved in a single step while (iii), (iv) and (v) may need multiple steps to be solved.\n\nThere are two methods commonly used to solve linear equations in one variable.",
