        "id": "8.4",
        "title": "8.4 The Solution of a Linear Equation in Two Variables",
        "theory": "8.4 The Solution of a Linear Equation in Two Variables\n\nConsider the following linear equation,\n\n2x-y=4\n\nPut x = 1,\n\n2(1)−y=4\n\n2-y=4\n\n-y=4-2\n\n-y=2\n\ny=-2\n\n[By multiplying both sides by-1]\n\nNote it down\n\nA linear equation in two variables does\n\nnot possess a\n\nunique solution.\n\nA linear equation in\n\none variable\n\npossesses a unique\n\nCheck:\n\nWe\n\n(x, z) = (1,-2)\n\n2x-y=4\n\nPut x = 1 and y=-2\n\n2 (1)-(-2) = 4\n\n2+2=4\n\n4 = 4\n\nsay that the equation 2x - y = 4 is satisfied by x = 1 and y = -2. Hence (1,-2) is a solution of this equation. Now, again consider this equation.\n\nPut x = 2,\n\n2x-y=4\n\n2(2)-y=4\n\n4-y=4\n\n-y=4-4.\n\n-y=0\n\ny=0\n\n(x, y) = (2, 0)\n\nIf we check this solution by substituting values of x and y in the given equation, we find that the equation 2x-y= 4 is also satisfied by these values of x and y. Hence (2, 0) is also a solution of the\n\nequation\n\n2x -y=4. This reveals that a linear equation in two variables is satisfied by more than one\n\n142)\n\npairs of values of the variables involved. Further investigations prove that:\n\nA linear equation in two variables is satisfied by an infinite\n\nnumber of pairs of values of the variables involved.\n\nSome more values of x and y which satisfy the equation 2r-y=4 have been displayed in the following table.\n\nI\n\nJ\n\n-2\n\nN\n\nThis table displays only a few values of x and y that satisfy the equation 2x-y= 4. In fact, there are innumerous values of x and y that satisfy this equation. This result is also true for all linear equations in two variables.\n\nQuick Check\n\n1. Which equations are linear\n\nequations?\n\n2. Which equations are\n\nsimultaneous equations.",
