        "id": "8.5",
        "title": "8.5 Cartesian coordinate system",
        "theory": "8.5 Cartesian coordinate system\n\nThe Cartesian coordinate system is a two-dimensional plane, called the cartesian plane. It is formed by two perpendicular lines. The horizontal line is called the x-axis and the vertical line is called the y-axis. The point where the two axes intersect is called the origin.\n\nNote it down\n\nThe cartesian plane is also called rectangular plane or XY-plane.\n\nOrigin\n\nB\n\nY-axis\n\nX-axis\n\n7-6-5-4-\n\n6 7\n\nD\n\nOrdered Pair\n\nThe numbers that are used to represent a point are called coordinates (x coordinate and y coordinate). An ordered pair consists of the x coordinate (called abscissa) and the y coordinate (called ordinate). An ordered pair represents two values written in sequence (x, y) within parentheses. In the figure, two ordered pairs are plotted in the cartesian plane.\n\nY-axis\n\n4,4)\n\n(7.5)\n\nX-axis\n\nH\n\nQuadrants\n\nThe axis are perpendicular to each other (i.e. meeting at 90°). The x and y axes divides the whole plane into 4 sections. These 4 sections are called \"quadrants\", namely Quadrant I, Quadrant II, Quadrant III, Quadrant IV.\n\nThe following are the characteristics:\n\nQuadrant I: positive x, positive y\n\n(+,+) Quadrant II: negative x, positive y (+) Quadrant III: negative x, negative y (-) Quadrant IV: positive x, negative y (+-)\n\nNote it down\n\nThe ordered pair for the origin is always (0,0).\n\nNote it down\n\nIn an ordered pair, the first number in an ordered pair tells us to move left or right from the origin. The second number tells us to move up or down.\n\nКОЛЕНИЯ\n\nQuadrant II\n\nY-axis\n\n(− +)\n\nQuadrant 1\n\n|(+ '+)\n\nX-axis\n\n7-6-5-4-3-2\n\n2 3 4 5 6 7\n\nQuadrant III\n\n(-7)\n\nQuadrant IV (+,−)\n\n2 axis).\n\nNote it down\n\nThe Quadrants from I to IV go in anticlockwise\n\ndirection in the coordinate plane.\n\nUse prerequisites like number lines to introduce the horizontal axis (x-axis) and the vertical axis (y-\n\nIdentifying the unknown point in the coordinate plane Example 1:\n\nIdentify the quadrant in which Point A is located. Then locate find the order pair for point A.\n\nSolution:\n\nThe point A is in the First quadrant, so x and y both will be positive in the required ordered. pair.\n\nStart at the origin (0, 0). Count along the x-axis until you exactly under the point A.\n\nBIC. y\n\nY-axis\n\nAlxy\n\nX-axis\n\nWe can see that the x-coordinate here is 5. Here 5 indicates that you have moved four units. to the right(positive) along the x-axis. This is the x-coordinate, (the first number in the ordered pair).\n\nNext, from 5 on the x-axis move up to the point and look for the number with which it aligns on the y-axis. Here we can see that it along with 3 on y-axis. Here 3 indicates that you have moved three units up(positive) along the y-axis. This is the y-coordinate, (the second number in the ordered pair). So, the x-coordinate 5 and y-coordinate 3 makes the ordered pair (5, 3).\n\nExplain that the order of the numbers in ordered pair matters a lot and they must note that the point (2,5) has a different position in the cartesian plane as compared to the point (5, 2). Similarly, the -ve and +ve numbers also have different positions.\n\n146)\n\nSimilarly, the ordered pair for point B can be found in the same way. Point B is in the 2nd quadrant, so here the x-coordinate will be negative and y-coordinate will be positive. Following the same steps, we can be found that coordinates of point B are (-7, 5).\n\nY-axis\n\nB(-7,5)\n\nA(5, 3)\n\nX-axis\n\n7-6-5-4-3-2\n\n2 3 4 5 6 7\n\n+3\n\n-6\n\n\"Note it down\n\nThe x-coordinate is the value in a pair of coordinates that shows how far along the point is on the x-axis. The x-coordinate is always written first in an ordered pair of coordinates (x, y).\n\nNote it down\n\nThe y-coordinate is the value in a pair of coordinates that shows how far along the point is on the y-axis. The y-coordinate is always written in the second position in an ordered pair of coordinates (x, y).\n\nPlotting the ordered pairs in the coordinate plane\n\nFollow these simple steps to plot the order pairs in coordinate plane: To plot the ordered pairs in the coordinate plane, follow these steps:\n\nStep 1: Take square or graph paper.\n\nStep II: Draw the x-axis and y-axis and label them.\n\nAlso label the origin O(0,0).\n\nStep III: Identify the quadrant in which the ordered pair is located.\n\nStep IV: Start at the origin (0,0) and plot the x-coordinate by counting the correct\n\nunits in the correct direction and quadrant.\n\nStep V: Mark the point and mention the ordered pair.",
        "heading": "8.5 Cartesian coordinate system",
