        "id": "we-7-16",
        "title": "Example 1",
        "problem": "Plot a graph for the equation 3x + y = 2.",
        "solution": "We will find a few pairs of numbers for x and y which satisfy the equation 3x + y = 2. Now for the required graph, the value of x will always be -2.\nPutx = 1 in 3x + y = 2;\n3(1) + y = 2\n3(1)+y=2\n3+ y = 2\ny = 2-3\ny=-1\nPut x = -2 in 3x + y = 2;\n3(-2) + y = 2\n-6+y=2\n-6+y=2\ny=2+6\ny = 8\nPut x=-1 in 3x+y=2;\n3(-1) + y = 2\n-3+y=2\ny=2+3.\ny=5.\nPut.x = 3 in 3x + y = 2;\n3(3) + y = 2\n9+ y = 2\ny = 2-9\ny =-7\nX\n-2\n-1\n<-1\n-7\nSo, some of the ordered pairs for the above equation are:\n(1,-1), (-2, 8), (1, 5), (3, -7),\nLet's plot all these ordered pairs to get the required graph.\nY-axis\nX-axis\n-5\n-2 -1\nThis is the required graph for the equation 3x + y = 2.\n155)"
