        "title": "9.2.1 Circumference of a cirlce",
        "theory": "9.2.1 Circumference of a cirlce\n\nA circle is a plain figure whose boundary consists of points equidistant from\n\nits centre.\n\n. Radius\n\nDiameter\n\n. Circumference.\n\ncircumference\n\nWe know that the perimeter of a square is the total length of its boundary. Similarly the total length of the boundary of the circle is called the circumference of the circle. Ratio between Circumference of a Circle and its Diameter\n\nConsider three circles with the following values of\n\nradius and circumference.\n\n9.4\n\nMath History\n\nEarly Greeks used\n\nthe value 22 for pi\n\nArchimedes\n\n(250 BC) proved that the value of pi\n\nlies between 3+ and 3-0\n\nFor Circle A = 94\n\nCircle A: Circumference = 9.4 cm,\n\nDiameter = 3 cm\n\n= 3.14 (approx.)\n\nCircle B: Circumference = 18.8 cm,\n\nDiameter = 6 cm\n\nFor Circle B = 18.8 = 3.14 (approx.)\n\nCircle C: Circumference 21.9 cm,\n\n=\n\nDiameter = 7 cm\n\nFor Circle C = 21.9 = 3.14 (approx.)\n\n219-3.14\n\nWe can see that the quotient of circumference and diameter of a circle is approximately the same i.e.3.14. We call this value \"Pi\" shown by symbol . It is the ratio between the circumference of a circle to the diameter. The value of always remains constant.\n\nSo,\n\ncircumference C\n\ndiameter\n\n등\n\nQuick Check\n\nor Circumference = x diameter\n\nC = лd\n\nWe know that diameter = 2 x radius\n\nSo,\n\nd = 2r\n\nC=яxd\n\nC = n2r or 2xr\n\nDraw three circles with different radii. Next note their diameter, length of the circumference and ..\n\nCircumference\n\nDiameter Circumference Diameter\n\nAsk the students to trace any round object onto a plain piece of paper. Then take a thread and\n\nmeasure the circumference.",
        "heading": "9.2.1 Circumference of a cirlce",
        "headingUrdu": "",
        "text": "9.2.1 Circumference of a cirlce\n\nA circle is a plain figure whose boundary consists of points equidistant from\n\nits centre.\n\n. Radius\n\nDiameter\n\n. Circumference.\n\ncircumference\n\nWe know that the perimeter of a square is the total length of its boundary. Similarly the total length of the boundary of the circle is called the circumference of the circle. Ratio between Circumference of a Circle and its Diameter\n\nConsider three circles with the following values of\n\nradius and circumference.\n\n9.4\n\nMath History\n\nEarly Greeks used\n\nthe value 22 for pi\n\nArchimedes\n\n(250 BC) proved that the value of pi\n\nlies between 3+ and 3-0\n\nFor Circle A = 94\n\nCircle A: Circumference = 9.4 cm,\n\nDiameter = 3 cm\n\n= 3.14 (approx.)\n\nCircle B: Circumference = 18.8 cm,\n\nDiameter = 6 cm\n\nFor Circle B = 18.8 = 3.14 (approx.)\n\nCircle C: Circumference 21.9 cm,\n\n=\n\nDiameter = 7 cm\n\nFor Circle C = 21.9 = 3.14 (approx.)\n\n219-3.14\n\nWe can see that the quotient of circumference and diameter of a circle is approximately the same i.e.3.14. We call this value \"Pi\" shown by symbol . It is the ratio between the circumference of a circle to the diameter. The value of always remains constant.\n\nSo,\n\ncircumference C\n\ndiameter\n\n등\n\nQuick Check\n\nor Circumference = x diameter\n\nC = лd\n\nWe know that diameter = 2 x radius\n\nSo,\n\nd = 2r\n\nC=яxd\n\nC = n2r or 2xr\n\nDraw three circles with different radii. Next note their diameter, length of the circumference and ..\n\nCircumference\n\nDiameter Circumference Diameter\n\nAsk the students to trace any round object onto a plain piece of paper. Then take a thread and\n\nmeasure the circumference.",
