        "theory": "9.4 Cylinder\n\nIn the previous class we have learnt about cylinders. We know that a cylinder is a 3-D shape which has two circular flat ends. Beakers, pipes, cans, buckets, candles, etc. are examples of cylinders.\n\nThere are more than one type of cylinder, but we will discuss only the right circular cylinder here. A right circular cylinder is the most commonly discussed type of cylinder. In such cylinders, the line segment joining the centres of the circular faces is perpendicular to the base. There are 3 faces of a cylinder, 2 are flat circular faces with the same radius and one is a curved surface. Circular faces of a cylinder are parallel to each other.",
