        "theory": "9.4.1 Surface Area of a Cylinder\n\nConsider a cylinder of radius rand height h.\n\nThe total surface area is the sum of the\n\narea of the curved surface and flat surfaces.\n\nNote it down\n\nThe perpendicular distance (altitude or height) of any shape from base to the opposite vertex is the shortest distance from the base.\n\nThe flat faces are circular, so the area of each circular flat face is nr.\n\nArea = r2\n\nд\n\nTo find the surface area of the curved surface, let's take an empty paper roll and cut it along the vertical line as shown in the figure. We can see that it is rectangular in shape. Now the width of this rectangle is h (height of cylinder) and length is the circumference of the circular face of the cylinder i.e. 2xr. So, Area of rectangle = h x 2лr = 2xrh\n\nSo, the area of curved surface of a cylinder= 2xrh.\n\nC = 2xr\n\nh\n\nArea = h x 2xr\n\n2 -\n\nThe total surface area of a cylinder = Area of flat faces + Area of curved surface.\n\nTotal surface area of a cylinder = 2 x яr2 + 2лrh\n\n= 2x2 + 2xrh\n\n= 2πr(r+h)\n\nTake an empty tissue role and ask the students to cut it as the above procedure. Now using the formula, find its surface area and share their findings with their class-fellows.",
