            "question": "Draw the lines of symmetry for each of the five pictured quadrilaterals, from left to right (p.205).",
            "solution": "(a) The rotated square has four lines of symmetry: its two diagonals and the two lines joining midpoints of opposite sides. Turning a square does not change its symmetry. (b) The non-isosceles trapezium has no line of symmetry. (c) The intended isosceles trapezium has one line: the perpendicular bisector of its parallel bases. (d) The ordinary oblique parallelogram has no line of symmetry. (e) The kite has one line through the two vertices where equal adjacent sides meet. To check a proposed line, reflect the whole outline across it: both halves must coincide.",
