            "solution": "(a)(iii) Diameter: it joins two circle points through the centre and is the longest chord. (b)(i) Points: every point of a circle is the same distance from its centre. (c) The source wording is ambiguous. The standard theorem 'angle in a semicircle' means the angle at a circumference point subtended by a diameter; that angle is90°, option(i), right. The angle at the centre spanning a semicircle is180°, option(iv), straight. These are different angles; use the intended angle-in-a-semicircle theorem for the book's answer. (d)(iv) An ordinary oblique parallelogram has no reflection line. Special parallelograms such as squares, rectangles and rhombuses do have lines, so this choice refers to the ordinary pictured/type example. (e)(i) Trapezium, using this book's exactly-one-parallel-pair definition. (f)(ii) Convex polygon: none of its interior angles exceeds180°; a concave polygon has a reflex interior angle."
