        "title": "12.5 Finding Unknown Angles of a Triangle",
        "theory": "Activity from printed p.233: draw a triangle on paper; mark its three angles with different colours; cut out the three corners; place them next to one another on a straight line. Together they form a straight angle, illustrating that a+b+c=180°.\n\nThe three interior angles of every triangle add to 180°. If two angles are known, subtract their sum from 180° to find the third angle.\n\nAn exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle form a straight line and add to 180°. The exterior angle also equals the sum of the two remote interior angles. These facts can be combined with vertically opposite angles, which are equal, to solve diagrams.",
