            "solution": "Use the local markings of each small triangle. (a),(b): triangle DBC has equal-side marks on DB, DC and BC, and its angle at D is60°. It is equilateral, so a=b=60°. (c),(d): EF=ED and angle FED=30°, so the equal base angles are c=d=(180°−30°)/2=75°. (e): triangle GHF has angle G=45° and angle H=90°, so e=180°−45°−90°=45°. (f),(g): triangle IHJ has matching side marks and angle I=60°, so f=g=60°. (h): triangle KJL has60° at J and60° at L; h=180°−60°−60°=60°. (i): triangle MLN has30° at M and90° at L; i=180°−30°−90°=60°. (j): triangle ANB has70° at N and70° at B; j=180°−70°−70°=40°. The marked central angle LON is vertically opposite the given40° angle FOD, so it is40°. Source consistency note: these answers use each indicated local triangle. The full drawing contains conflicting extra markings. For example, M,L,O are drawn on one line and the right angle at L makes angle OLN90°, but the equal marks LO=ON would make triangle LON have two right angles, which is impossible. Thus the whole star cannot satisfy every printed mark simultaneously; the local calculations above should not be treated as proof that the entire drawing is geometrically consistent.",
