        "id": "13.3",
        "title": "13.3 Pie Graph",
        "theory": "A pie graph is a circle divided into sectors. The full circle represents the total quantity, and each sector represents one part. The sector angle is proportional to that part's share of the total.\n\nTo read a sector, use \\(\\text{part}=\\frac{\\text{sector angle}}{360^\\circ}\\times\\text{total}\\). Its percentage is \\(\\frac{\\text{sector angle}}{360^\\circ}\\times100\\%\\).\n\nA pie graph represents4320 books sold in a bookstore during six weeks. The sector angles for weeks1–6 are35°,45°,22°,42°,126°,90°. (a) What is the graph about? (b) In which week were most books sold? (c) In which week were least books sold? (d) What percentage of the total books was sold in the fourth week?\n\n(a) The chart shows how4320 book sales were distributed over six weeks. (b) Week5: its126° sector is largest. (c) Week3: its22° sector is smallest. (d) Week4 has42° out of360°. Its book count is \\(\\frac{42}{360}\\times4320=504\\). Its percentage is \\(\\frac{504}{4320}\\times100=11\\frac23\\%\\approx11.7\\%\\). As a check, each degree represents4320/360=12 books, so weekly counts are420,540,264,504,1512,1080; their sum is4320. The angles total35+45+22+42+126+90=360°.\n\nTo construct a pie graph, first add all observations. Then calculate each sector angle using \\(\\theta=\\frac{\\text{category value}}{\\text{total}}\\times360^\\circ\\). Draw a circle and radius, use a protractor to mark successive central angles, and label the sectors. All sectors must together make360°. The school-fee contribution example and expenditure Quick Check provide worked constructions.",
