        "id": "13.6.1",
        "title": "13.6.1 Arithmetic Mean",
        "theory": "The arithmetic mean is found by adding all observations and dividing by the number of observations. It uses every value in the dataset.\n\nIf the observations are represented by x and there are n of them, \\(\\bar{x}=\\frac{\\sum x}{n}\\). The symbol \\(\\sum x\\), read as sum of x, means the total of all observations. Rearranging gives \\(\\sum x=n\\bar{x}\\): total = number of observations × mean.\n\nExample 1: find the mean of 1, 6, 8, 9, 7, 5. There are n=6 observations. Their sum is 1+6+8+9+7+5=36. Hence \\(\\bar{x}=\\frac{36}{6}=6\\).\n\nExample 2: the average weight of four boys is 24 kg. With a fifth boy included, the mean becomes 24.6 kg. Find the fifth boy's weight.\n\nThe four boys' total weight is 4×24=96 kg. Let the fifth boy weigh y kg. The five boys' total is 96+y kg, so \\(24.6=\\frac{96+y}{5}\\). Multiply both sides by 5: 123=96+y. Subtract 96: y=27 kg. Check: (96+27)/5=123/5=24.6 kg. The printed initial n=6 is a typo: there are four boys initially and five after the addition.",
